    "part": "PART III — FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS",
    "title": "Article 19: Protection of Certain Rights Regarding Freedom of Speech, etc.",
    "definition": "(1) All citizens shall have the right to — (a) freedom of speech and expression; (b) assemble peaceably and without arms; (c) form associations or unions; (d) move freely throughout the territory of India; (e) reside and settle in any part of the territory of India; (f) [omitted by 44th Amendment — right to acquire, hold, and dispose of property]; (g) practise any profession, or to carry on any occupation, trade, or business. (2)-(6) Reasonable restrictions on these rights may be imposed by law in the interests of sovereignty and integrity of India, security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, contempt of court, defamation, incitement to an offence, and other specified grounds.",
    "explanation": "Article 19 is the magna carta of individual liberties in the Indian Constitution, guaranteeing six fundamental freedoms to every citizen of India. Originally containing seven freedoms, the right to property under Article 19(1)(f) was deleted by the 44th Constitutional Amendment in 1978, reducing the count to six. These freedoms are not absolute — each is subject to \"reasonable restrictions\" that the State may impose through law on specified grounds enumerated in Clauses (2) through (6).\n\nThe architecture of Article 19 is distinctive. Clause (1) enumerates the freedoms in sub-clauses (a) through (g). Each subsequent clause — (2) through (6) — specifies the permissible grounds on which the State can restrict the corresponding freedom. This structure creates a self-contained framework for balancing individual liberty with collective interests.\n\n**Article 19(1)(a) — Freedom of Speech and Expression:** This is the most extensively litigated and judicially developed freedom. The Supreme Court has interpreted it expansively to include freedom of the press, the right to information, the right to silence, the right to advertise, the right to broadcast, the right to telecast, the right to access the internet, and even the right to fly the national flag. In Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras (1950), the Court established that freedom of speech is the foundation of democratic governance.\n\nThe reasonable restrictions under Article 19(2) include sovereignty and integrity of India, security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, contempt of court, defamation, and incitement to an offence. The phrase \"sovereignty and integrity of India\" was added by the 16th Amendment in 1963, in response to secessionist movements.\n\nThe landmark Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015) struck down Section 66A of the IT Act for violating Article 19(1)(a), establishing important principles about online speech. The Court distinguished between \"discussion,\" \"advocacy,\" and \"incitement,\" holding that only the last can be restricted.\n\n**Article 19(1)(b) — Right to Assemble Peaceably:** Citizens have the right to hold public meetings, demonstrations, and rallies, subject to the conditions that they must be peaceful and the participants must be unarmed. Reasonable restrictions can be imposed in the interests of sovereignty and integrity of India or public order.\n\n**Article 19(1)(c) — Right to Form Associations or Unions:** This includes the right to form political parties, trade unions, societies, clubs, and companies. However, it does not include the right to strike — the Supreme Court in T.K. Rangarajan v. Government of Tamil Nadu (2003) held that government employees have no fundamental right to strike.\n\n**Article 19(1)(d) and (e) — Freedom of Movement and Residence:** These twin freedoms guarantee every citizen the right to move freely and to reside anywhere in the territory of India. They were designed to prevent internal barriers to movement and ensure national integration. Reasonable restrictions can be imposed in the interests of the general public or for the protection of Scheduled Tribes.\n\n**Article 19(1)(g) — Freedom of Profession, Occupation, Trade, or Business:** This guarantees every citizen the right to practise any profession or carry on any business. The State can impose reasonable restrictions in the interest of the general public, including professional qualifications, licensing requirements, State monopolies, and regulations.\n\nThe concept of \"reasonable restrictions\" is the balancing mechanism of Article 19. The Supreme Court in Chintaman Rao v. State of Madhya Pradesh (1951) established that the restriction must be reasonable both substantively (not excessive or disproportionate) and procedurally (imposed by law, not arbitrary executive action). The restriction must have a direct and proximate nexus with the permitted ground.\n\nArticle 19's transformation through Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978) was revolutionary. The Court held that Articles 14, 19, and 21 are not watertight compartments but form a \"golden triangle\" — any law restricting a fundamental right must satisfy all three articles simultaneously. This integrated reading ensures comprehensive protection of individual liberty.",
